From 225399d2fccb5d8c22f6cbe01300d21a79fcf288 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 15:42:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 218/226] webui: QoS - warn that the download cap can hurt
 real-time traffic

RQOS_117 (download-cap toggle description) sold the ingress policer as all-upside. Add the trade-off: it is off by default, and a policer drops rather than queues, so enabling it can add packet loss to latency-sensitive real-time traffic (video/voice calls). English source only; 24-language translation rides the next i18n pass (native review owed, per the project i18n norm).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 release/src/router/www/EN.dict | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/release/src/router/www/EN.dict b/release/src/router/www/EN.dict
index 86782fde76..cd8e75f0f5 100644
--- a/release/src/router/www/EN.dict
+++ b/release/src/router/www/EN.dict
@@ -5585,7 +5585,7 @@ RQOS_113=Pick a device or enter a MAC address (AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF).
 RQOS_114=Download and upload must each be at least 0.1 Mb/s.
 RQOS_115=You can limit up to 32 devices.
 RQOS_116=Download cap (ingress policer)
-RQOS_117=Polices WAN ingress so heavy downloads back off before the ISP buffer fills &mdash; download bufferbloat control with hardware acceleration still on. Enter your <b>measured</b> download speed (from a speed test); the router automatically keeps ~10% headroom, so the cap holds full rated speed with lower ping. A policer at your full line rate would collapse throughput, so that headroom is applied for you &mdash; no manual 90% math. Burst sizing is automatic.
+RQOS_117=Polices WAN ingress so heavy downloads back off before the ISP buffer fills &mdash; download bufferbloat control with hardware acceleration still on. Enter your <b>measured</b> download speed (from a speed test); the router automatically keeps ~10% headroom, so the cap holds full rated speed with lower ping. A policer at your full line rate would collapse throughput, so that headroom is applied for you &mdash; no manual 90% math. Burst sizing is automatic. <b>Off by default:</b> a policer <i>drops</i> excess rather than queuing it, so turning this on can add packet loss to latency-sensitive real-time traffic such as video and voice calls &mdash; enable it only for download bufferbloat control when you accept that trade-off.
 RQOS_118=Wi-Fi downstream priority (WMM)
 RQOS_119=Stamps DiffServ onto LAN-bound traffic for the top classes &mdash; Q1 to CS6 (voice airtime), Q2 to CS5 (video airtime) &mdash; so Wi-Fi serves your priorities on the download side too. Lift-only: nothing is ever demoted below normal best-effort. Wired clients are unaffected; a connection keeps the class it started with.
 RQOS_120=How does the download side work?
